# ⚠ VERIFIED INERT ON THIS HOST (2026-08-13). Deployed, then probed with a
# cache-busting query to force a Cloudflare MISS: the origin still returned
# max-age=14400, so these directives never ran. GO54 puts nginx in front of
# Apache and nginx serves static files itself, which means .htaccess is not
# consulted for a .apk at all. Changing the APK's cache lifetime needs either a
# Cloudflare Cache Rule on /download/*.apk or a host-side nginx change — it
# cannot be done from this repo. Kept only so the next person does not spend the
# same hour rediscovering that.
#
# The working defence is the versioned filename (download/hawkeye-<version>.apk):
# a URL that has never held older bytes cannot be served stale. Bump it on every
# rebuild.
#
# The APK must not sit in a cache after it is rebuilt.
#
# The host's mod_expires gives application/vnd.android.package-archive a 4-hour
# max-age (HTML gets max-age=0). That is how a debug build Play Protect blocks
# kept being installed for hours after it had been replaced: origin bytes were
# correct on every check, and the stale copy lived downstream at a CDN edge and
# in the phone's own download cache.
#
# Five minutes with must-revalidate: still spares the origin a re-download for
# anyone retrying a failed install, but a rebuild reaches people almost at once.
# Versioned filenames (download/hawkeye-<version>.apk) remain the primary
# defence; this is the backstop for when someone forgets to bump the name.
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.apk$">
    ExpiresActive On
    ExpiresDefault "access plus 5 minutes"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.apk$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
